{"name":"Field Poppy","hex":"#d86f3c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"field poppy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["the common poppy (Papaver rhoeas), native to western Eurasia and north Africa."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/field_poppy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Papaver rhoeas","blocks":[{"text":"Papaver rhoeas, with common names including common poppy, corn poppy, corn rose, field poppy, Flanders poppy, red poppy, and Odai, is an annual herbaceous species of flowering plant in the poppy family Papaveraceae. It is native to north Africa and temperate Eurasia and is introduced into temperate areas on all other continents except Antarctica."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Papaver_rhoeas_Lincolnshire_2026.jpg/330px-Papaver_rhoeas_Lincolnshire_2026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papaver_rhoeas"},{"title":"Poppy","blocks":[{"text":"A poppy is a flowering plant in the subfamily Papaveroideae of the family Papaveraceae. Poppies are herbaceous plants, often grown for their colourful flowers. One species of poppy, Papaver somniferum, is the source of the narcotic drug mixture opium, which contains powerful medicinal alkaloids such as morphine and has been used since ancient times as an analgesic and narcotic medicinal and recreational drug. It also produces edible seeds. Following the trench warfare in the poppy fields of Flanders, Belgium, during World War I, poppies, specifically the red poppy, have become a symbol of remembrance of soldiers who have died during wartime, especially in the UK, Canada, Australia, Africa and other Commonwealth realms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Poppies_in_the_Sunset_on_Lake_Geneva.jpg/330px-Poppies_in_the_Sunset_on_Lake_Geneva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poppy"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Papaver_rhoeas_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-101.jpg/330px-Papaver_rhoeas_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-101.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Papaver_rhoeas_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-101.jpg/330px-Papaver_rhoeas_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-101.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKoeh-101.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Poster_papaver_3a.jpg/500px-Poster_papaver_3a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Poster_papaver_3a.jpg/1280px-Poster_papaver_3a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Poster_papaver_3a.jpg/500px-Poster_papaver_3a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The three stages in a common poppy flower: bud, flower, and capsule","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoster_papaver_3a.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Papaver_rhoeas_capsules.jpg/500px-Papaver_rhoeas_capsules.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Papaver_rhoeas_capsules.jpg/1280px-Papaver_rhoeas_capsules.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Papaver_rhoeas_capsules.jpg/500px-Papaver_rhoeas_capsules.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Capsules","link":"https://en.wikipedia